# Shell scripts must keep LF endings on every platform.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is common on Windows workstations, and it rewrites text files to CRLF on
# checkout. For a script that runs on the Linux deployment host that is not cosmetic: the shebang
# becomes `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, which the kernel resolves to a program named "bash\r", and the
# script fails to start with a message that points nowhere near the real cause.
#
# Deliberately narrow. This pins the executable text that has to run somewhere other than the
# machine it was checked out on; it is not a general line-ending policy for the repository.
*.sh text eol=lf
